          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient assessed as moderate suicide risk is being prepared for discharge. Which of the following represents the most evidence-based approach to reducing risk between clinical contacts?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Obtaining a signed no-suicide contract from the patient before discharge</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No-suicide contracts lack an evidence base and may generate false reassurance. They treat the patient's verbal commitment as a safety guarantee rather than addressing the underlying crisis with a concrete plan.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Collaboratively developing a written, personalised safety plan with the patient</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A safety plan is an evidence-based intervention constructed jointly with the patient, detailing personalised coping strategies, warning signs, and contacts to use when suicidal urges intensify.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Prescribing a higher dose of anxiolytics to suppress suicidal urges at home</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Increasing anxiolytic doses without a structured crisis plan is not evidence-based and may paradoxically increase risk through disinhibition or by providing additional means of self-harm.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Documenting the assessment findings thoroughly without any patient-facing intervention</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Documentation is one of the three core applied responses, but it is a clinician-facing record, not a patient-facing intervention. It must be accompanied by safety planning and, where appropriate, means restriction.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the three core applied responses after a suicide risk assessment: safety planning (collaborative, written, personalised crisis plan), means restriction, and accurate documentation. The safety plan is the key patient-facing intervention — no-suicide contracts are not interchangeable with it."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Safety planning is collaboratively constructed, personalised, and evidence-based — it is explicitly distinct from and clinically superior to a no-suicide contract, which has no evidence base."
                }
